{
  "dom_id": "step-q73072",
  "name": "Question 7",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14252554/assets/68379dee045e8b5d4f123f91c0f8c953d2cbbe6475c5ca13d4435889343c625d.png)\n\nThe graphs of the functions $y=f(x)$ and $y=g(x)$ are given above. Find ${h}^{′}(-1.5)$ where $h(x)=f(x)g(x).$",
  "worked_solution": "The product rule for derivatives gives\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(x)={f}^{′}(x)\\cdot g(x)+f(x)\\cdot {g}^{′}(x).\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have that\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(-1.5)={f}^{′}(-1.5)\\cdot g(-1.5)+f(-1.5)\\cdot {g}^{′}(-1.5).\n$$\n\nFrom the graphs above, we obtain that $f(-1.5)=-0.5$ and $g(-1.5)=-0.5.$\n\nAdditionally:\n\n- The slope of $f(x)$ on $x\\in (-2.5,0)$ equals $1.$ So, ${f}^{′}(-1.5)=1.$\n\n- The slope of $g(x)$ on $x\\in (-3,-0.5)$ equals $0.$ So, ${g}^{′}(-1.5)=0.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{′}(-1.5) & ={f}^{′}(-1.5)\\cdot g(-1.5)+f(-1.5)\\cdot {g}^{′}(-1.5) \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-0.5)+(-0.5)\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =-0.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
